I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the provided diffraction-analysis tooling first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build and refine a multiphase GSAS-II model, and verify that the exported CIFs, profile, fractions, statistics, and `final.gpx` all agree.

The chemistry narrows the likely inventory to copper tungstate plus residual CuO/WO₃ or a Cu–W–O intermediate, but I won’t assume that from synthesis alone. I’m now extracting the observed peak families and checking the frozen COD/reference-search and GSAS-II interfaces so phase acceptance is driven by indexed reflections and full-profile refinement.

The peak-family test has separated three coherent components:

- triclinic CuWO₄ (COD 4000806) accounts for the dominant 18.81°, 25.46°, 29.09°, 30.08°, 31.48°, 35.62°, and 36.53° families;
- cubic Cu₃WO₆ (COD 1527404) accounts independently for 15.18°, 19.79°, 32.49°, 33.77°, 49.69°, 57.61°, and 63.28°;
- a low-symmetry WO₃ family accounts for the 22.67°/23.07°/23.89° triplet and its higher-angle partners.

This is also reaction-balanced: Cu-rich Cu₃WO₆ requires a W-rich phase in a nominal 1:1 Cu:W batch. I’m moving these three into comparable staged whole-pattern refinements and will test whether any component’s scale collapses or leaves a coherent residual family.

The three-phase model is stable: after allowing only shared angular corrections and phase cells, all three scales remain substantial (about 65% CuWO₄, 15% Cu₃WO₆, 20% WO₃), and Rwp has already fallen from 18.9% to 12.1%. The current angular zero/height correlation is high, so I’ll later constrain that pair using the full-angle trend rather than treating either number alone as physical. The refinement is continuing through instrumental and phase-specific broadening.

A genuinely different WO₃ coordinate start (COD 2311041, same monoclinic topology) reproduces the WO₃ family materially better than COD 1528915 under the identical refinement sequence: Rwp 4.82% versus 5.99%, with all three phase scales still stable. That is evidence for the 2311041-type WO₃ coordinates, not an extra phase. I’ll now refine each accepted phase’s coordinates separately, beginning with heavy atoms and preserving rollback points whenever geometry or profile parameters become nonphysical.

The remaining dominant residual is now a separate, coherent precursor family rather than a profile error: 35.09°, 38.31°, 48.44°, 53.04°, 58.11°, 61.21°, and 67.67° all track tenorite CuO under the refined common displacement. I’m adding CuO as a fourth phase and will require its scale to remain stable after cell/profile/coordinate relaxation. This is the only new phase family indicated; the other residuals coincide with already accepted reflections.

CuO is decisively retained: after adding it, Rwp dropped from 3.89% to 2.07%, and its refined abundance stabilized near 2.3 wt% rather than collapsing. Its coordinate refinements gave no reproducible improvement, so I’m retaining the published tenorite coordinates. The residuals now all sit on accepted phase reflections; I’m scanning symmetry-allowed preferred-orientation axes phase by phase to resolve their remaining intensity imbalance without inventing another phase.

The refined four-phase fractions are now 65.7% CuWO₄, 12.8% Cu₃WO₆, 19.1% WO₃, and 2.3% CuO. Independently of the fit, those fractions close the nominal Cu:W balance to about 0.3%, which strongly supports both the inventory and quantification. Common per-phase Uiso refinement lowered Rwp to 1.99% (the counting-statistical minimum reported by GSAS-II is about 1.62%). I’m checking only phase-specific anisotropic broadening and specimen effects now; library search has stopped because no coherent reflection family remains unindexed.

The phase assignment is closed at four crystalline phases. I’m finishing the physical-coordinate check—especially the weakly constrained WO₃ oxygen substructure—then I’ll export and independently replay the exact final project and deliver all requested files.

The current four-phase model is physically sound (shortest WO₃ W–O 1.726 Å; shortest O–O 2.478 Å), and its weighted residual is at the counting-noise level. I’m doing one final convergence replay of the WO₃ oxygen coordinates because that is the least constrained part of the model; I will retain it only if the gain and geometry remain stable.

The structural refinement is now converged: final Rwp = 1.7271%, GOF = 1.071, and the last maximum shift/esd is 0.015. The refined crystalline fractions are 65.56% CuWO₄, 12.79% Cu₃WO₆, 19.22% WO₃, and 2.43% CuO. I’m now regenerating the pattern from the saved atoms and profile parameters, ignoring GSAS-II’s cached calculated curve, as the final consistency check.

The independent replay passed cleanly: it found the original 8,301-point scan, regenerated all four atomic phases without optimizing anything, reproduced the submitted fractions exactly, and raised no physical-profile flags. I’m completing the final file-integrity checklist now.

